<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<!-- When creating a new activity, make a copy of this file with appropriate name -->
<worksheet xml:id="activity-06-sensitivity1">
    <title>06 - Sensitivity Analysis Part 1</title>

    <!-- Optional introduction -->
    <introduction>
        <p>
            In this activity we want to analyze how sensitive a model is to its
            parameters.
        </p>
    </introduction>
    <!-- Exercises start here. -->
    <exercise>
        <introduction>
            <p>
                The population of Zika mosquitoes can be modeled with the
                function
                <md>
                    P(t) = 8000\left(\frac{1}{6} + e^{-\frac{(t-c)^2}{d}} + e^{-\frac{(t-9)^2}{6}}\right)
                </md>
                where <m>t</m> is given in months.
                We will investigate the effect of the parameters <m>c</m> and
                <m>d</m> on the model.
            </p>
        </introduction>

        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Create a Google Colab document that plots the model for
                    <m>{c = 3}</m> and <m>{d = 6}</m>.
                    Let <m>t</m> range from 0 to 12.
                    Define the function for your model to take <m>c</m> and
                    <m>d</m> as inputs, along with the variable <m>t</m>.
                    You might have this from the last worksheet!
                    Make sure to import the following packages:
                    <ul>
                        <li><c>import math</c></li>
                        <li><c>import numpy as np</c></li>
                        <li><c>import matplotlib.pyplot as plt</c></li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </statement>

            <solution component="06-solutions">
                <program language="python">
                    <input>
#import python libraries that will help us
import math
import numpy as np
import matplotlib.pyplot as plt

#Function definition  The key elements in it are the function name and parameters as well as the return variable.
def P(t,c,d):       # Creates definition of P(t) that later you can call for some defined 't', 'c' and 'd'. This is the function name.
    out = 8000*(1/6+np.exp(-(t-c)**2/d)+np.exp(-(t-9)**2/6))
    return out

# You've only defined your function, you haven't called it yet. You need to create an input.
t = np.linspace(0,12,2000)

# Choose your parameters
c=3
d=6

y = P(t,c,d)    # Run the model
plt.plot(t,y)   # Plot the model
plt.xlabel('time (months)')
plt.ylabel('population')
plt.title('Parameters c='+str(c) +' and d='+str(d))
plt.show()
                    </input>
                </program>

                <image source="images/06-1a.png" width="75%">
                    <shortdescription>
                        Graph of the population of Zika mosquitoes over time.
                    </shortdescription>
                </image>
            </solution>
        </task>

        <task workspace="1in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Let's start by seeing what happens when we change the
                    parameter <m>c</m> drastically.
                    Let <m>d = 6</m> as before.
                    Plot the population of mosquitos for
                    <m>{c = 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6}</m>.
                    Let's do this using a for loop.
                </p>

                <p>
                    Begin by creating a list of the <m>c</m> values using the
                    following command: <c>clist = np.arange(1,7,1)</c>.
                    This creates "clist" from 1 (inclusive) to 7 (exclusive)
                    with steps of size 1.
                </p>

                <p>
                    To create a legend for your plot that differentiates between
                    the <m>c</m> values, add a label to your plot line of code:
                    <c>plt.plot(t,y, label='c='+str(c))</c>.
                </p>

                <p>
                    What do you notice?
                    How does the model of the population change?
                </p>
            </statement>

            <solution component="06-solutions">
                <p>
                    This builds on the previous code from Part (a).
                </p>

                <program language="python">
                    <input>
# Define parameters, and c as an array of the desired parameters
clist = np.arange(1,7,1)       # create clist from 1 (inclusive) to 7 (exclusive) with steps of size 1
d=6

# For each number in clist, we want to run the model and plot it
for i in range(0,len(clist)):  # i is the index of the value in clist, 0 through 5
  c = clist[i]                 # define c as the correct element from clist
  y = P(t,c,d)
  plt.plot(t,y, label='c='+str(c))

plt.xlabel('time (months)')
plt.ylabel('population')
plt.title('Fix d='+str(d)+' and vary the parameter c')
plt.legend()                   # show the label of each plot in a legend
plt.show()
                    </input>
                </program>

                <image source="images/06-1b.png" width="75%">
                    <shortdescription>
                        Graph of the population of Zika mosquitoes over time
                        with varying the parameter c.
                    </shortdescription>
                </image>

                <p>
                    The graph changes drastically, where a higher value of
                    <m>c</m> actually merges the two "humps" of the graph and
                    creates one larger "hump." The maximum increases during this
                    merge.
                    The relative minimum also continues to move later in time
                    and at a higher population as the parameter <m>c</m> gets
                    larger.
                    However, the end behavior at the end of the year is
                    consistent across all values of <m>c</m>.
                </p>
            </solution>
        </task>

        <task workspace="1in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Let's look at what happens if rather than drastic changes in
                    <m>c</m>, we only change <m>c</m> by about <m>2\%</m>.
                    To do this, let's look at the outputs for
                    <m>{c = 2.88, 2.94,
                    3, 3.06, 3.12}</m>.
                    Let's keep track of the relative minimum in between the two
                    "humps" of the curve and compare how the output changes
                    based on how the input changed.
                    If the parameter <m>c</m> changes by 0.06 (about <m>2\%</m>
                    ), how much does the relative minimum of the population
                    change?
                </p>

                <p>
                    Some helpful things to consider:
                    <ul>
                        <li>numpy has the function <c>np.min()</c></li>
                        <li>
                            to find the percent change in the output at the
                            relative min, we take the difference in the current
                            output and the previous output values, divide by the
                            previous output, and turn it into a percentage
                        </li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </statement>

            <solution component="06-solutions">
                <p>
                    This builds on the previous code from Parts (a) and (b).
                </p>

                <program language="python">
                    <input>
clist=np.arange(2.88,3.13,.06)       # the parameters for c (start at 2.88 inclusive, stop at 3.13 exclusive, step size .06)
d = 6

cmin=np.zeros(5)                     # create zero vector that will later be filled with the relative minimum value for each 'c'

# Run the model for each c value, and find the relative minimum
for i in range(0,len(clist)):
    c=clist[i]
    y = P(t,c,d)
    plt.plot(t,y,label='c='+str(c))
    m= np.min(y[500:1500])           # Find the minimum of the output between the two humps
    cmin[i]=m                        # Fill the correct index entry of cmin with the min
    print('when c=',c,' the relative min is: ',m)
plt.xlabel('time')
plt.ylabel('population')
plt.legend()
plt.show()

## To find the percent change in the output at the relative min,
##   we take the difference in the output values, divide by the input and turn it into a %
for i in range(0,len(cmin)-1):       # For each index, but now we need to stop at 1 less than the total
    change = cmin[i+1]-cmin[i]       # Calculate the difference between i+1 and i index
    percent = change/cmin[i]*100     # Calculate the percent change
    print('The population output changed by: ',change,'which is ',percent,'%')
                    </input>
                </program>

                <image source="images/06-1c.png" width="75%">
                    <shortdescription>
                        Graph of the population of Zika mosquitoes over time
                        with varying the parameter c by 2 percent.
                    </shortdescription>
                </image>
            </solution>
        </task>

        <task workspace="1in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Based on this analysis, do you think the model is sensitive
                    to changes in the parameter <m>c</m>?
                </p>
            </statement>

            <solution component="06-solutions">
                <p>
                    Since the percent change in the output is about the same as
                    the percent change in the input, this model is not sensitive
                    to the change in the parameter <m>c</m>.
                </p>
            </solution>
        </task>

        <task workspace="1in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Now let's look at the affect of changing the parameter
                    <m>d</m>.
                    Let's begin by changing the parameter <m>d</m> drastically.
                    Let <m>c = 3</m> as we did at the beginning.
                    Plot the population of mosquitos for
                    <m>{d = 4, 5, 6, 7, 8, 9}</m>.
                </p>

                <p>
                    What do you notice?
                    How does the model of the population change?
                </p>
            </statement>

            <solution component="06-solutions">
                <p>
                    This builds on the previous code from Part (a).
                </p>

                <program language="python">
                    <input>
# Define parameters, and d as an array of the desired parameters
c = 3
dlist = np.arange(4,10,1)       # create dlist from 4 (inclusive) to 10 (exclusive) with steps of size 1

# For each number in dlist, we want to run the model and plot it
for i in range(0,len(dlist)):  # i is the index of the value in dlist, 0 through 5
  d = dlist[i]                 # define d as the correct element from dlist
  y = P(t,c,d)
  plt.plot(t,y, label='d='+str(d))

plt.xlabel('time (months)')
plt.ylabel('population')
plt.title('Fix c='+str(c)+' and vary the parameter d')
plt.legend()                   # show the label of each plot in a legend
plt.show()
                    </input>
                </program>

                <image source="images/06-1e.png" width="75%">
                    <shortdescription>
                        Graph of the population of Zika mosquitoes over time
                        with varying the parameter d.
                    </shortdescription>
                </image>

                <p>
                    The graph has about the same shape, but the starting values
                    are different, as well as the relative minimum at around 6
                    months.
                    There is also a slight shift earlier for the relative
                    minimum in time.
                </p>
            </solution>
        </task>

        <task workspace="1in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Let's look at what happens if rather than drastic changes in
                    <m>d</m>, we only change <m>d</m> by about <m>2\%</m>.
                    To do this, let's look at the outputs for
                    <m>{d = 5.76, 5.88, 6, 6.12, 6.24}</m>.
                    Let's again keep track of the relative minimum in between
                    the two "humps" of the curve and compare how the output
                    changes based on how the input changed.
                    If the parameter <m>d</m> changes by 0.12 (about <m>2\%</m>
                    ), how much does the relative minimum of the population
                    change?
                </p>
            </statement>

            <solution component="06-solutions">
                <p>
                    This builds on the previous code from Parts (a) and (e).
                </p>

                <program language="python">
                    <input>
c=3
dlist=np.arange(5.76,6.25,.12)       # the parameters for d

dmin=np.zeros(5)                     # create zero vector that will later be filled with the relative minimum value for each 'd'

# Run the model for each d value, and find the relative minimum
for i in range(len(dlist)):
    d=dlist[i]
    y = P(t,c,d)
    plt.plot(t,y,label='d='+str(d))
    m= np.min(y[500:1500])           # Find the minimum of the output between the two humps
    dmin[i]=m                        # Fill the correct index entry of cmin with the min
    print('when d=',d,' the relative min is: ',m)
plt.xlabel('time')
plt.ylabel('population')
plt.legend()
plt.show()

## Find the percent change in the output at the relative min
for i in range(len(dmin)-1):         # For each index, but now we need to stop at 1 less than the total
    change = dmin[i+1]-dmin[i]       # Calculate the difference between i+1 and i index
    percent = change/dmin[i]*100     # Calculate the percent change
    print('The population output changed by: ',change,'which is ',percent,'%')
                    </input>
                </program>

                <image source="images/06-1f.png" width="75%">
                    <shortdescription>
                        Graph of the population of Zika mosquitoes over time
                        with varying the parameter d by 2 percent.
                    </shortdescription>
                </image>
            </solution>
        </task>

        <task workspace="1in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Based on this analysis, do you think the model is sensitive
                    to changes in the parameter <m>d</m>?
                </p>
            </statement>

            <solution component="06-solutions">
                <p>
                    No, the model is not sensitive to changes in the parameter
                    <m>d</m> since the percent change in the output is smaller
                    than the percent change in the input.
                </p>
            </solution>
        </task>

        <task workspace="1in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Is the model more sensitive to changes in the parameter
                    <m>c</m> or <m>d</m>?
                </p>
            </statement>

            <solution component="06-solutions">
                <p>
                    The percent change in the output when changing the parameter
                    <m>c</m> by <m>2\%</m> is about 2 times larger than the
                    percent change in the output when changing the parameter
                    <m>d</m> by <m>2\%</m>.
                    Therefore, the model is more sensitive to the parameter
                    <m>c</m>.
                    Although, again, it is not particularly sensitive to either
                    of the parameters.
                </p>
            </solution>
        </task>
    </exercise>

    <exercise>
        <introduction>
            <p>
                Assume we introduce a new species to an area and monitor the
                population over 24 years.
                Let <m>t</m> be the number of years after the introduction of
                the new species, and <m>f(t)</m> be the number of animals in the
                area at time <m>t</m>.
                Assume also that <m>f(t)</m> is modeled fairly well by
                <md>
                    f(t) = -\frac{1}{4}(at)^3 + 6t^2 + 10t
                </md>
                where <m>a</m> is an unknown parameter.
                However, when we fit the model to data we find that <m>a</m> is
                about 1.
                We want to determine if our model is sensitive to the parameter
                <m>a</m>.
            </p>
        </introduction>

        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Plot the model with <m>a = 1</m> and show the first 24
                    years.
                    Then plot the model for <m>{a = 0.99}</m> and
                    <m>{a = 1.01}</m>.
                    Put all three plots on the same graph.
                    Notice that there is a <m>1\%</m> change in the parameter
                    <m>a</m>.
                </p>
            </statement>

            <solution component="06-solutions">
                <program language="python">
                    <input>
#import python libraries that will help us
import math
import numpy as np
import matplotlib.pyplot as plt

def f(t,a):      # the function defined with inputs both t and a
  out = -1/4*(a*t)**3+6*t**2+10*t
  return out

# You've only defined your function, you haven't called it yet. You need to create an input.
t = np.linspace(0,24,2000)

# Choose your parameter
alist=np.arange(0.99,1.01,0.01)

fend=np.zeros(3)

for i in range(len(alist)):
  a=alist[i]
  y = f(t,a)
  m= y[-1]         # Find the value of f after t = 24 years
  fend[i]=m
  print('when a=',a,' the value of f after 24 years is: ',m)
  plt.plot(t,y,label='a='+str(a))

plt.xlabel('time (years)')
plt.ylabel('population')
plt.legend()
plt.show()

## Find the percent change in the output at 24 years
for i in range(len(fend)-1):         # For each index, but now we need to stop at 1 less than the total
    change = fend[i+1]-fend[i]       # Calculate the difference
    percent = change/fend[i]*100     # Calculate the percent change
    print('The population output changed by: ',change,'which is ',percent,'%')
                    </input>
                </program>

                <image source="images/06-2a.png" width="75%">
                    <shortdescription>
                        Graph of the population of a new species over 24 years.
                    </shortdescription>
                </image>
            </solution>
        </task>

        <task workspace="1in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Determine the percent change in the output of the model at
                    the end of the time period, 24 years.
                    That is, when <m>a</m> is changed by <m>1\%</m>, how much
                    does the population change by at the end of 24 years?
                </p>
            </statement>

            <solution component="06-solutions">
                <image source="images/06-2a.png" width="75%">
                    <shortdescription>
                        Graph of the population of a new species over 24 years.
                    </shortdescription>
                </image>
            </solution>
        </task>

        <task workspace="1in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    In your opinion, is this model sensitive to the parameter
                    <m>a</m>?
                    Why or why not?
                </p>
            </statement>

            <solution component="06-solutions">
                <p>
                    Yes!
                    The model is very sensitive to the parameter <m>a</m>!
                    There is a huge percent change in the ending value with only
                    a small percent change in the input parameter value.
                    Note: the percent change is negative, which means that there
                    is a decrease in the output.
                    However, when we are thinking about the impact of the
                    parameter change, the negative sign isn't important compared
                    to the size of the percent change.
                </p>
            </solution>
        </task>
    </exercise>
</worksheet>